 \begin{abstract}
-A decision-maker whose deployed policy reshapes its own data distribution must pay, in its own objective, to learn how it moves the world. We price that self-knowledge exactly. In a structural performative market (a dealer whose quoted spread summons the adverse flow it must then price), we prove a conservation law: along any stationary exploration policy, the variance of the response-slope estimate times the cumulative performative regret equals $\tfrac{1}{2}\gpo\sigma^2$, an invariant of the environment rather than the policy. Around the identity: minimax floors (exact for deviation budgets, constant-factor for value budgets); a structure-proofness theorem with an exact reach (within two curvature lengths of the anchor the floor survives full knowledge of the response family; just beyond, a verified design beats it); an optimal-design theory (explore as $\Gpo^{-1/2}$; isotropy overpays by an explicit dispersion factor); and a deployable agent, SafeD-PerfGD, which corrects only when an anytime confidence sequence certifies, via a perturbed-modulus lemma, that the correction cannot destabilize the retraining loop. Every closed form is verified in a live deploy--observe--fit--correct pipeline (36 preregistered checks, 0 failures), transplanted to a second economy, and instantiated on the REFLEX corporate-bond calibration \citep{reflex2026}, reproducing its published run 10/10 cells. Code, derivations, and all checks ship as an anonymized repository in the supplementary material.
